<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calabar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calabar is a port city in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>. After the British expedition of 1897 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovonramwen">Oba Ovonramwen</a> was exiled to Calabar. He died there in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calabar.jpg" alt="Calabar" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MediaMOF · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hand_sculpture_roundabout,_Calabar,_Cross_River_state2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Calabar is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river-state">Cross River State</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It was originally named Akwa Akpa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efik">Efik</a> language, because the Efik people dominate the area. The city lies beside the Calabar and Great Kwa rivers and the creeks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a>'s inland delta. Portuguese explorers who reached this part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> coast in the 15th century called the peoples of the area Calabar, among them the Quas, Efuts and Efiks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since the 16th century Calabar has served as an international seaport, exporting goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>. In the centuries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> it became a major port for shipping enslaved Africans to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. From 1725 until 1750 roughly 17,000 enslaved Africans were sold from Calabar to European traders, and from 1772 to 1775 the number rose to more than 62,000. Old Calabar, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-town">Duke Town</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-town">Creek Town</a>, 16 kilometres to the north-east, were crucial to the trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> abolished the slave trade in 1807.</p><p>Scottish Presbyterians established a mission station in 1846. The missionaries Hope Waddell, who worked in Calabar from 1845 to 1858, and Mary Slessor, from 1876 to 1915, tried to change or abolish practices including the killing of newborn twins and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> at the deaths of elders. They influenced many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efik">Efik people</a> to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. On 10 September 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> signed a treaty of protection with the king and chiefs of Akwa Akpa, then known to Europeans as Old Calabar, and the city was the headquarters of European administration in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> until the seat of government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> in 1906.</p><p>Calabar is credited with several Nigerian firsts, among them a secondary school, the Hope Waddell Training Institution, and a hospital, St Margaret's. In 1922 the Legislative Council was created, and one of its four elected members was from Calabar, among the first Africans elected to a parliamentary body in British West Africa. In 1926 an attempt to introduce a poll tax led to the protests called the Women's War by Nigerians, and the riots spread to Calabar, where more than 50 women died at the hands of colonial forces. In October 1967 Nigerian forces captured the city from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Calabar covers 406 square kilometres and is divided into the Calabar Municipal and Calabar South Local Government Areas, which had 371,022 people in the 2006 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The National Museum, formerly the colonial governor's residence, was shipped in parts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1884 and became a national monument in 1959.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Calabar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This provided the pretext for Admiral Harry Rawson's Punitive Expedition, which burned Benin City, looted the palace and exiled Oba Ovonramwen to <b>Calabar</b>, where he died in 1914.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…from the Igbo hinterland were carried in large numbers to the ports of the Bight of Biafra, such as Bonny and <b>Calabar</b>, and the Aro, with their oracle at Arochukwu and their chain of trading settlements, built a new kind of power that ritual sanctions could not restrain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nri">Kingdom of Nri · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Efik settled on the <b>Calabar</b> river in the 17th century, founding Creek Town, Old Town and Duke Town, which Europeans called Old Calabar.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-calabar">Old Calabar</a></li></ul></section>
